[Music]  a way out is not the hard-hitting  emotional tale of two convicts escaping  prison it appears to be at times it  successfully strikes those nose but  extreme tonal shifts gimmicky cuties and  a terrible finale kill almost any  emotional tension contained in the game  in the end entertaining environment and  some inventive set pieces proved a bit  saving grace like director Joseph Farah  says last game brothers a tale of two  tons a way out contains two protagonists  who experience the story together  but unlike brothers you'll need a friend  to play with this time around a wire is  only playable in co-op either locally or  online whichever you choose you'll  always be playing in a split screen that  dynamically shifts between the  respective views of a reckless  aggressive gangster cliche named Leo and  a more cool-headed family man in Vincent  this framing device is mostly used in  interesting ways such as giving more  screen space to whoever's performing a  more important action or splitting the  TV and three two also dedicate real  estate to an attacking NPC however it  can irritate like when my partner  triggered a cutscene while I was halfway  through exploring or when you both  engage in separate conversations meaning  two sets of dialogue overlap are you  saying it's all in my head but these  issues are more of a footnote than a  major strike against away outs co-op  only nature without a partner in crime  some of the game's standout moments  wouldn't feel as impactful in one early  scene Leo and Vincent are attempting to  hack away at their jail cells using a  screwdriver while your partner stabs the  wall behind his toilet you must keep  watch from your adjacent cell for  patrolling guards occupying them when  they get too close and warning the other  player to look natural when your  distraction fails I can't sleep do you  have a book I could read what do I look  like a librarian this is when a way out  is at its best  communicating with and relying on your  partner both in game and in real life  makes these moments of tension  consistently thrilling there are a  handful of these set pieces throughout  the seven to eight hour campaign that  feel unique and they justify the  decision of forcing you to play with  another person  but while those moments do carry some  tension it's because you're sat next to  someone you care about and never because  you're playing as someone you care for  the protagonists and their motivations  are the most generic b-movie fodder  gangsters with escape and revenge on  their minds but with the hackneyed added  layer of troubled families to make  matters worse the dialogue is stilted  and unnatural conversations often end  abruptly regardless of whether your  partner triggers a cutscene too early  and entire scenes go by without adding  anything in terms of plot or  characterization some lines in  particular are cringe-worthy during one  sequence in which a couple are  interrupted while having sex  a female extra instructs her male  partner to shut the door because the  tone veers wildly from a Shawshank  inspired escape tale - a silly semi  parody of 70s crime dramas complete with  overextended sideburns an assassination  across the border and a villains remote  Mexican lair in one scene away is  nailing the feel of punishing prison  life but in another it's letting you act  like children on a playground swing  sometimes those conflicting tones even  happen in parallel one poignant  late-game moment when my character  learned some surprising and emotional  news on one side of the screen was  ruined by my partner interacting with a  bicycle Bell on the other side that  caused his character to exclaim I need  to fly somewhere tomorrow if it's not  the dialogue dampening moments of  tension is the game's numerous QTEs  while away out does use time button  tapping well in some instances such as  when our characters must time there  pushes up a vent shaft while standing  back-to-back it also wastes scenes with  gimmicky implementations like mashing  square or X as quickly as possible  pure luck I slip away at its third and  fourth acts are by far its weakest save  for one inventive story beat all  creativity is lost and the game turns  into a mediocre action romp with anemic  shooting and little else to do or care  about  luckily the rest of the game which is  much longer than the mercifully  contracted finale contains more  interesting and varied environment these  diverse areas are small but dense and  the add color to what could otherwise be  a monochrome world of good and bad the  trailer park was a personal favorite  offering a chance to pause and play some  baseball nice or chat some secondary  characters but this captivating space  comes during what should be a time  sensitive moment in the plot when  playing baseball or mocking around on  swings would be the last things on  anyone's mind and this says everything  about a wet story in tone by the time  the credits rolled my partner and I  didn't really feel like we'd been on  much of a journey with Leo and Vincent  we'd been on a geographical tour sure  but we didn't feel like the pair had  learned anything or grown in any  meaningful way but I did enjoy the  journey I've been on with my friends sat  next to me we'd had to look out for each  other while escaping prison work  together to solve puzzles and save each  other's life for multiple occasions our  characters might not have grown closer  together but aware it's forced co-op is  worth it the few standout moments it  provides by the way what are you in for  something I didn't do so you're innocent  huh well aren't we all  I'm innocent too you know yeah I'm sure  you are

